# Firebase client configuration — REVIEWED AND RESTRICTED 2026-10-02.
#
# `android/app/google-services.json` and `ios/Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist`
# each carry a Google API key (`AIza...`). Both were reviewed against the rule
# at the top of this file before being allowlisted.
#
# Why these are not secrets: the keys are compiled into every APK and IPA that
# ships, so anyone who installs the app can extract them. Keeping them out of
# git protects nothing, because distribution publishes them regardless, and
# Google documents both files as safe to commit. Omitting them would instead
# break the build for any future contributor and silently disable Firebase via
# the conditional in android/app/build.gradle.kts.
#
# Why that alone was NOT considered sufficient: an UNRESTRICTED Google API key
# can call any API enabled on the project, and this Firebase project shares its
# GCP project with the Google OAuth client. An extracted key could burn quota
# or spend against a personally-funded account. So before allowlisting, both
# keys were given application restrictions in the Google Cloud Console —
# Android bound to package `in.sarovarjinalaya.app` plus the signing SHA-1,
# iOS bound to bundle id `in.sarovarjinalaya.app` — which makes an extracted
# copy unusable from anywhere else. That restriction, not this allowlist, is
# the actual mitigation.
#
# OUTSTANDING: the Android restriction currently covers only the DEBUG signing
# certificate (SHA-1 11:21:BC:9B:F1:FA:9C:D1:98:15:5A:2E:D6:22:52:14:36:1C:59:BB).
# When the Android upload keystore is created for a real release, that build is
# signed with a different key and WILL be blocked until its SHA-1 is added
# here too. This is the kind of restriction that works in testing and fails on
# the first Play Store build.
android/app/google-services.json:gcp-api-key:23
